# EXPERIMENT 7 – DEVELOP A CONTAINERIZED APPLICATION USING DOCKER

## AIM
To develop and run a containerized Event Registration application using Docker.

## THEORY
Containerization packages an application and its dependencies into a portable container.

A Dockerfile contains instructions to build a Docker image.

The image can then be used to create and run containers.

## STEP 1: Open the existing project

powershell
cd "C:\Users\Dell\Desktop\event-registration"


## STEP 2: Create Dockerfile

Create a file named exactly Dockerfile without an extension.

dockerfile
FROM node:24-alpine

WORKDIR /app

COPY package*.json ./

RUN npm install --omit=dev

COPY . .

EXPOSE 3000

CMD ["node", "server.js"]


## STEP 3: Create .dockerignore

text
node_modules
.git
.env


## STEP 4: Test the application locally

powershell
node server.js


Open:

text
http://localhost:3000


Stop the server using Ctrl+C.

## STEP 5: Build Docker image

powershell
docker build -t external-exp7 . .


*Expected output:* Successfully built image.

## STEP 6: Verify image

powershell
docker images


You should see event-registration.

STEP 7: Run the container
docker run -d --name exp7 -p 3001:3000 external-exp7


STEP 8: Verify container
docker ps


You should see the container named exp7 with port mapping 3001->3000.
STEP 9: Open the application
Open this URL in your browser:

http://localhost:3001

Test the Event Registration form.

To view API data:

http://localhost:3001/api/registrations
STEP 10: View logs
docker logs exp7


STEP 11: Stop and start container
Stop the container:
docker stop exp7


Start the container again:
docker start exp7


Verify:
docker ps


STEP 12: Remove container
docker stop exp7
docker rm exp7


Important: Keep the Docker image external-exp7. You will need a Docker image for your Kubernetes experiments. Do not run docker rmi.
RESULT
The Event Registration application was successfully containerized and executed using Docker.
## VIVA
1. What is a Dockerfile?  
   It is a text file containing instructions to build a Docker image.

2. What is FROM?  
   It specifies the base image.

3. What is WORKDIR?  
   It sets the working directory inside the image.

4. What is COPY?  
   It copies files into the image.

5. What is RUN?  
   It executes a command during image building.

6. What is EXPOSE?  
   It documents the port used by the application.

7. What is CMD?  
   It specifies the default command to run when the container starts.

8. What does -p 3001:3000 mean?  
   Host port 3001 is mapped to container port 3000.

